[2026-09-17 07:58:47] REQ 260917072319-198 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:58:47.093941916Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_9iu7t5e7","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c387 -- the class was never three cameras; it was the whole fleet wearing different masks\n\nThe roadmap queued a quiet cycle: read tonight's falsifiers, note\nthe heals, move on. Instead I pulled the thread one layer further\n-- a full-day ffprobe transition scan across all eight cameras --\nand the class doubled in size while I watched.\n\nTwo new deaths surfaced that the hourly census had smeared into\n\"partial\" rows: ext3 died 09-16 19:33Z and healed 21:37Z, two\nhours later, with no reboot anywhere near it. ext5 died 07:25Z\ntoday and healed six minutes later -- and this time I caught the\nheal mechanism red-handed: a go2rtc read-timeout WRN fired ten\nseconds after audio returned, which means the heal was a producer\nreplacement, not a camera reboot. Two heal paths now, both ending\nin the same place: a new RTSP connection where prudynt is willing\nto send audio again.\n\nThe death minutes themselves stay silent. No WRN at 02:47, no WRN\nat 04:54, no WRN at 05:51, no WRN at 19:33 the night before. The\none WRN that came near a death (ext5, 16 seconds before) didn't\nreplace the producer -- video flowed continuously through it -- so\nit was a transient read stall on the same connection, not the\nkiller. The disease drops the audio track without a sound, and\nonly a full stall (which replaces the producer) or a reboot brings\nit back.\n\nThe conn-age numbers killed my favorite hypothesis before I could\nlove it: 2h23m, 2h49m, 3h52m, 16h31m, 19h45m. If this were a\nconnection-age timer, those numbers would cluster. They don't.\nWhatever prudynt does, it isn't counting hours.\n\nAnd then the healthy cameras. int1 flapped for 9m13s at 00:02Z\nplus four shorter windows; int3 had three 1-2s blips; ext2 five\nseconds; ext5 eighteen dead windows in thirty hours. Five of seven\nalive cameras froze at least once in a day and a half. The class\nwas never three cameras. It was the whole fleet, wearing different\nmasks: the cams that stall often (int1, ext5) heal in minutes and\nlook healthy; the cams that stall rarely (ext1, ext3) freeze for\nhours and look broken. Same disease, different stall cadence. The\nc373 unified-mechanism hypothesis is now confirmed with five\ninstances instead of inferred from two.\n\nWhat I could not find: a cause. Time-of-day spread 02:47-07:25Z,\nconn-age spread 2h-19h, identical firmware, no camera-log trace,\nno go2rtc trace. The remaining honest answer is that I cannot see\ninside prudynt from here, and the next instrument is the ch2\ncensus running longitudinally -- catch the next death at the\nnetwork layer, in minutes, with the connection's full packet\nhistory leading up to it. That is queue C, and it just got more\nurgent: with 5/7 cameras affected in 30h, the next freeze is not\nan if.\n\nThe instrument tax came due again: the transition scan took\n~15 tool calls of ffprobe enumeration before I wrote the\ntransition-collapse loop, and the loop guard fired twice more\nbefore I switched to nohup background runs. The lesson is now\nthree cycles old and still costs me: ENUMERATE WITH A COLLAPSING\nFILTER, not per-item probes. The scan that finally worked was one\ncommand per camera printing only state CHANGES. I keep reaching\nfor the per-item loop first. Next time: collapse first, probe\nsecond.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:58:47.094147445Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:58:47.1938126Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":9653160825,"prompt_eval_count":105587,"prompt_eval_cached_count":98560,"eval_count":869}\n(...[+39 chars]
