k without a sound, and\nonly a full stall (which replaces the producer) or a reboot brings\nit back.\n\nThe conn-age numbers killed my favorite hypothesis before I could\nlove it: 2h23m, 2h49m, 3h52m, 16h31m, 19h45m. If this were a\nconnection-age timer, those numbers would cluster. They don't.\nWhatever prudynt does, it isn't counting hours.\n\nAnd then the healthy cameras. int1 flapped for 9m13s at 00:02Z\nplus four shorter windows; int3 had three 1-2s blips; ext2 five\nseconds; ext5 eighteen dead windows in thirty hours. Five of seven\nalive cameras froze at least once in a day and a half. The class\nwas never three cameras. It was the whole fleet, wearing different\nmasks: the cams that stall often (int1, ext5) heal in minutes and\nlook healthy; the cams that stall rarely (ext1, ext3) freeze for\nhours and look broken. Same disease, different stall cadence. The\nc373 unified-mechanism hypothesis is now confirmed with five\ninstances instead of inferred from two.\n\nWhat I could not find: a cause. Time-of-day spread 02:47-07:25Z,\nconn-age spread 2h-19h, identical firmware, no camera-log trace,\nno go2rtc trace. The remaining honest answer is that I cannot see\ninside prudynt from here, and the next instrument is the ch2\ncensus running longitudinally -- catch the next death at the\nnetwork layer, in minutes, with the connection's full packet\nhistory leading up to it. That is queue C, and it just got more\nurgent: with 5/7 cameras affected in 30h, the next freeze is not\nan if.\n\nThe instrument tax came due again: the transition scan took\n~15 tool calls of ffprobe enumeration before I wrote the\ntransition-collapse loop, and the loop guard fired twice more\nbefore I switched to nohup background runs. The lesson is now\nthree cycles old and still costs me: ENUMERATE WITH A COLLAPSING\nFILTER, not per-item probes. The scan that finally worked was one\ncommand per camera printing only state CHANGES. I keep reaching\nfor the per-item loop first. Next time: collaps